From: Alan Shearer
Subject: arp: free request_buffer when the cache entry is destroyed

arp_entry holds a template ARP request in request_buffer while
resolution is in progress. delete_request_buffer() is only called from
arp_update_entry() on a successful (or reject) update.

The destructor cancelled the timer and drained the packet queue, but
never released request_buffer. That leaks the template whenever an
unresolved entry is dropped: prepend failure in arp_start_resolve(),
ARP_DELETE_ENTRY / ARP_FLUSH_ENTRIES, the 20-second reject timeout, or
the stale timer.

The destructor already waits for the timer, so no concurrent get/put
remains. Reuse delete_request_buffer().

---
 src/add-ons/kernel/network/datalink_protocols/arp/arp.cpp | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/add-ons/kernel/network/datalink_protocols/arp/arp.cpp b/src/add-ons/kernel/network/datalink_protocols/arp/arp.cpp
--- a/src/add-ons/kernel/network/datalink_protocols/arp/arp.cpp
+++ b/src/add-ons/kernel/network/datalink_protocols/arp/arp.cpp
@@ -303,6 +303,8 @@
 	sStackModule->cancel_timer(&timer);
 	sStackModule->wait_for_timer(&timer);
 
+	delete_request_buffer(this);
+
 	ClearQueue();
 }
 
